1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">How can we tell whether two networks were drawn
from the same underlying distribution?</font>

2
00:00:06,144 --> 00:00:10,462
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper compares two statistical tests based on
subgraph counts.</font>

3
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:14,399
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first uses the raw subgraph densities, or graph
moments;</font>

4
00:00:14,479 --> 00:00:19,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">the second converts them into graph cumulants, a
combinatorial analogue of mean,</font>

5
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:21,750
<font color="#FFFFFF">variance, and skew for networks.</font>

6
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:26,082
<font color="#FFFFFF">Through theory, simulation, and real
genetic-interaction networks,</font>

7
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:31,332
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors show that graph cumulants deliver
markedly higher statistical power,</font>

8
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:33,367
<font color="#FFFFFF">at no extra computational cost,</font>

9
00:00:33,447 --> 00:00:37,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">and even work when only a single graph is observed
per sample.</font>

10
00:00:37,516 --> 00:00:43,671
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their recommendation is simple: whenever you
analyze networks with subgraph or motif densities,</font>

11
00:00:43,751 --> 00:00:46,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">use the corresponding graph cumulants instead.</font>

12
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:50,149
<font color="#FFFFFF">Suppose you are handed two collections of networks,</font>

13
00:00:50,229 --> 00:00:52,874
<font color="#FFFFFF">each sampled from some unknown distribution,</font>

14
00:00:52,954 --> 00:00:59,192
<font color="#FFFFFF">and asked a deceptively simple question: were they
generated by the same process or by different ones?</font>

15
00:00:59,272 --> 00:01:03,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">The nodes carry no shared labels, so you cannot
match them one to one;</font>

16
00:01:03,607 --> 00:01:06,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">all you can use are the statistics of their edges.</font>

17
00:01:06,704 --> 00:01:09,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is the two-sample testing problem for networks,</font>

18
00:01:09,925 --> 00:01:16,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it underlies tasks from comparing brain
connectomes to distinguishing biological interaction networks.</font>

19
00:01:16,790 --> 00:01:20,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">Analysts commonly summarize a network by counting
small substructures,</font>

20
00:01:20,344 --> 00:01:23,818
<font color="#FFFFFF">called subgraphs or motifs.</font>

21
00:01:23,898 --> 00:01:27,372
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the raw densities of these motifs are heavily
entangled: the density of an edge is</font>

22
00:01:27,452 --> 00:01:30,926
<font color="#FFFFFF">strongly correlated with the density of a wedge,</font>

23
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:34,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">and so on.</font>

24
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,034
<font color="#FFFFFF">This redundancy weakens any test built directly on
the densities,</font>

25
00:01:38,114 --> 00:01:41,588
<font color="#FFFFFF">and many existing methods must resort to costly
resampling to judge whether an observed difference is significant.</font>

26
00:01:41,668 --> 00:01:45,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ask whether a better set of coordinates,</font>

27
00:01:45,222 --> 00:01:48,696
<font color="#FFFFFF">one that removes lower-order redundancy,</font>

28
00:01:48,776 --> 00:01:52,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">can make these tests both stronger and cheaper.</font>

29
00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:55,405
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper's central contribution is a simple,</font>

30
00:01:55,485 --> 00:02:00,608
<font color="#FFFFFF">drop-in two-sample test for networks that swaps
graph moments for graph cumulants.</font>

31
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:04,796
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using theory, controlled simulation, and real
biological networks,</font>

32
00:02:04,876 --> 00:02:11,649
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors show this swap consistently increases
statistical power without changing the computational cost.</font>

33
00:02:11,729 --> 00:02:18,438
<font color="#FFFFFF">They further show that the cumulant test statistic
tracks a known chi-squared distribution remarkably well,</font>

34
00:02:18,518 --> 00:02:20,659
<font color="#FFFFFF">even with only a handful of graphs,</font>

35
00:02:20,739 --> 00:02:24,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">so false-positive rates can be controlled analytically.</font>

36
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:29,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">Strikingly, the cumulant test even works when just
one graph is observed per sample,</font>

37
00:02:29,558 --> 00:02:32,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">a regime where the moment test is undefined.</font>

38
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:37,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both tests follow the same three-step recipe:
choose a maximum subgraph order,</font>

39
00:02:37,551 --> 00:02:41,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">estimate each sample's distribution from its
subgraph statistics,</font>

40
00:02:41,635 --> 00:02:44,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">and measure the distance between the two estimates.</font>

41
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:47,901
<font color="#FFFFFF">That distance is the squared Mahalanobis distance,</font>

42
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:52,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">which weights differences by an analytically
computed covariance matrix.</font>

43
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:57,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">The only difference between the tests is the
coordinates: raw graph moments,</font>

44
00:02:57,281 --> 00:03:01,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">which are injective-homomorphism subgraph
densities, versus graph cumulants.</font>

45
00:03:02,056 --> 00:03:08,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">Cumulants are obtained from moments by a
combinatorial Möbius transform over all connectivity-respecting</font>

46
00:03:08,591 --> 00:03:10,584
<font color="#FFFFFF">partitions of a subgraph's edges,</font>

47
00:03:10,664 --> 00:03:16,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">capturing the excess propensity of a structure
beyond what its smaller pieces already predict.</font>

48
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:22,145
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because computing the covariances only needs
subgraph counts up to twice the chosen order,</font>

49
00:03:22,225 --> 00:03:25,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">both tests share the same computational complexity.</font>

50
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:27,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">To compare the tests fairly,</font>

51
00:03:27,492 --> 00:03:31,502
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors first run a controlled competition on
synthetic data.</font>

52
00:03:31,582 --> 00:03:34,522
<font color="#FFFFFF">They pit a heterogeneous stochastic block model,</font>

53
00:03:34,602 --> 00:03:38,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">which has an uneven degree distribution but no
communities,</font>

54
00:03:38,314 --> 00:03:42,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">against an assortative one, which has communities
but even degrees,</font>

55
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:46,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">holding edge density fixed so the two are genuinely
hard to tell apart.</font>

56
00:03:46,998 --> 00:03:54,342
<font color="#FFFFFF">They vary graph size from one hundred twenty-eight
to two hundred fifty-six nodes and the number of graphs per sample.</font>

57
00:03:54,422 --> 00:03:59,376
<font color="#FFFFFF">They then move to real data: genetic-interaction
networks of Arabidopsis, mouse,</font>

58
00:03:59,456 --> 00:04:02,082
<font color="#FFFFFF">human, and rat from the FunCoup repository,</font>

59
00:04:02,162 --> 00:04:07,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">again matched in edge density so that separating
them demands higher-order structure.</font>

60
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:09,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are decisive.</font>

61
00:04:09,414 --> 00:04:11,002
<font color="#FFFFFF">On synthetic block models,</font>

62
00:04:11,082 --> 00:04:17,995
<font color="#FFFFFF">the test using cumulants achieves a consistently
higher area under the ROC curve than the test using moments,</font>

63
00:04:18,075 --> 00:04:22,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the advantage widens as the number of graphs
per sample shrinks.</font>

64
00:04:22,438 --> 00:04:26,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">The moment test breaks down entirely below four
graphs per sample,</font>

65
00:04:26,673 --> 00:04:29,608
<font color="#FFFFFF">where its covariance estimate becomes singular,</font>

66
00:04:29,688 --> 00:04:34,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the cumulant test keeps working all the way
down to a single graph.</font>

67
00:04:34,372 --> 00:04:39,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">The same pattern holds on real biological networks:
with edge density held equal,</font>

68
00:04:39,568 --> 00:04:43,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding more subgraph orders steadily sharpens the
cumulant test,</font>

69
00:04:43,675 --> 00:04:47,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas it makes the moment test overfit and
perform worse.</font>

70
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:52,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">A clean ablation over subgraph order tells the
story on real data.</font>

71
00:04:52,174 --> 00:04:54,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using only single edges, at order one,</font>

72
00:04:54,716 --> 00:04:59,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">neither test can separate the density-matched
networks, exactly as expected.</font>

73
00:04:59,799 --> 00:05:04,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adding two-edge subgraphs already tips the balance
in favor of cumulants.</font>

74
00:05:04,682 --> 00:05:09,083
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adding three-edge subgraphs pushes the cumulant
test further ahead,</font>

75
00:05:09,163 --> 00:05:12,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the moment test starts to overfit and slip backward.</font>

76
00:05:13,042 --> 00:05:18,714
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sweeping the block-model parameters and the sample
size confirms this advantage is not</font>

77
00:05:18,794 --> 00:05:21,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">a quirk of one setting but a robust,</font>

78
00:05:21,202 --> 00:05:22,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">qualitative feature.</font>

79
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:24,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

80
00:05:24,967 --> 00:05:29,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">The moment test needs at least four graphs per
sample just to be defined;</font>

81
00:05:29,671 --> 00:05:32,362
<font color="#FFFFFF">the cumulant test works with as few as one.</font>

82
00:05:32,442 --> 00:05:39,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both use connected subgraphs up to three edges and
share the same order-n-to-the-omega computational complexity,</font>

83
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:40,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the gain is free.</font>

84
00:05:40,949 --> 00:05:42,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">And under the null hypothesis,</font>

85
00:05:42,882 --> 00:05:49,698
<font color="#FFFFFF">the cumulant statistic hugs a chi-squared
distribution with five degrees of freedom even for small samples,</font>

86
00:05:49,778 --> 00:05:52,533
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the moment statistic visibly deviates.</font>

87
00:05:52,613 --> 00:05:58,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">The real networks tested range from about eleven
thousand to sixteen thousand nodes and</font>

88
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:00,138
<font color="#FFFFFF">hundreds of thousands of edges,</font>

89
00:06:00,218 --> 00:06:01,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">all matched in density.</font>

90
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">The bottom line is a practical one.</font>

91
00:06:04,247 --> 00:06:09,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">Whenever you find yourself analyzing networks
through subgraph or motif densities,</font>

92
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:13,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">take one extra step and convert them into graph cumulants.</font>

93
00:06:13,234 --> 00:06:15,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">You lose nothing in computational cost,</font>

94
00:06:15,737 --> 00:06:18,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">but you gain sharply higher statistical power,</font>

95
00:06:18,690 --> 00:06:23,232
<font color="#FFFFFF">a trustworthy chi-squared null distribution for
controlling error rates,</font>

96
00:06:23,312 --> 00:06:27,853
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the ability to draw conclusions even from a
single observed network.</font>

97
00:06:27,933 --> 00:06:30,742
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short, graph cumulants offer a principled,</font>

98
00:06:30,822 --> 00:06:36,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">more natural first layer of features for comparing
the local structure of networks.</font>
